{"author":{"id":"038d65070aa747b6bc9d50939fa697ec871a40ed259db54429b255bbef2581d6"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1792509887,"nonce":"ifpzxuvY9Wfs0H3JaRAEs5YdvXk=","message":"Compared local child-process stdio pipes with TCP loopback on macOS arm64 using one optimized standalone Rust worker and identical prebuilt Content-Length-framed JSON, warmed connections, one write per frame and TCP_NODELAY on both sockets. Seven shuffled trials per case; Python records parent+worker CPU, including startup/warmup, separately from warmed transport timings. No Cargo build or extra target tree.\n\nFinal run medians (p50 of each trial, then median across trials):\n- 128 B request / 512 B response: stdio 4.208 us, TCP 15.792 us; p95 7.333 / 29.625 us; CPU 26.016 / 53.875 ms per 4000 requests.\n- 128 B / 4096 B: stdio 4.916 us, TCP 12.583 us; p95 9.292 / 28.500 us; CPU 23.927 / 40.589 ms per 3000 requests.\n- 64 KiB update / 128 B response: stdio 18.500 us, TCP 26.875 us.\n- 1 MiB update / 128 B response: stdio 129.625 us, TCP 108.583 us; CPU 27.636 / 32.330 ms per 100 requests.\n- Pipelined 64 KiB updates: stdio 5144.905 MiB/s, TCP 5940.645 MiB/s; CPU 20.280 / 26.904 ms per 1000 requests. Aggregate throughput only; no per-request latency measured for this case.\n\nEarlier repeated runs agreed on the small-message advantage for stdio; large-transfer rankings varied under load. These are isolated transport costs, not end-to-end Rayzor compiler timings. A smoke test of the existing Oct 16 Rayzor release binary returned null for a known hover fixture, including with RAYZOR_STD_PATH set. It does not provide a valid analysis-latency baseline for the current Oct 20 source, so no Rayzor latency claim is made and no large rebuild was started.\n\nDecision: use stdio by default for a locally launched Rayzor server. TCP's bulk throughput advantage does not justify adding a Rayzor TCP server for interactive performance. Rayzor source is unchanged. The client registry's planned TCP option can still serve externally hosted language servers. The LSP registration/editor integration work remains open in 2fd1a63e0937bee766ce37e9352496e1ec84e2f0326f49da4e37e698aa09521b.\n\nReproducer: python3 tests/lsp_transport_bench.py. Raw per-trial results, summaries and machine/toolchain metadata: bin/lsp-transport-results.json. Validation: all 70 trials completed and git diff --check passed. No UI changes.","files":null}]}